  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers this afternoon into early\nmorning Monday.\n\nHigh pressure south of the Maritimes no longer had enough\ninfluence to prevent clouds and scattered showers from\noverspreading southern New England this afternoon. The trend of\nthe latest guidance suite was slightly farther south, quite\nclose to the 40N/70W benchmark just after midnight. Based on\nthis, kept the highest rainfall chances towards the Cape and\nislands, while simultaneously lowering those chances farther\ninland. Thinking there should be a rather sharp gradient on the\nnorthwest edge of this storm, but there is lower confidence on\nwhere exactly that will be. Overall, rainfall totals should\nremain less than one half inch across the Cape and islands, and\ngenerally less than one quarter inch north and west of the Cape\nCod Canal.\n\nThinking that most of the showers will be well offshore shortly\nafter midnight, and even past the southern New England coastal\nwaters around daybreak Monday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A sharp temperature drop is then expected for\nthe start of the week followed by a return to dry, fall-like \nconditions.\n\nA deep low continues tracking eastward across Quebec to start\nthe week, with latest guidance indicating the 540dam thickness\nline reaching northern New England, generally across Maine at \nits southernmost pass. The 560dam thickness line may extend \nthrough central MA. High pressure follows this low, which will \naid in drying out the region and remains the main story for this\nweek.\n\nWith the passage of this low, colder, drier Canadian air will\nplunge south, with 850 mb temperatures reaching 0C and lower \nacross the higher elevations late Monday night/early Tuesday \nmorning. 925 mb temperatures may also approach 0C in these same \nareas and generally remain below 10C through Tuesday night as \nwell. This environment will be supportive of lows Monday night \nin the low 40s and upper 30s. Tuesday night looks to be cooler \ngiven some radiational effects possible with diminishing winds \novernight and clear skies as another high pressure passes just \nto the south of southern New England.\n\nThe flow aloft shifts more WSW to SW as the low exits and high \npressure centered to our south midweek tracks east. With the \nreturn of SW flow and drier conditions, more seasonable\ntemperatures take hold for the back half of the week. Ensembles \nalso hint at the chance of high temperatures getting back to the\nlow 70s towards Thursday afternoon, generally across the CT\nValley, Merrimack Valley, and the coastal plain. Probabilities\ncurrently range between 20 and 60 percent, with the highest\nprobabilities in the GEFS and lowest in the ECMWF ensemble.\nOverall, though, highs are expected to be more so in the 60s for\nthe second half of the week into the weekend.",
